### They want to break a painful or disruptive cycle of holding poop and repeated cleanup.

2 of 12 posts.

Parents of children aged two and a half and three and a half describe bowel problems that dominate daily life and postpone potty learning. One reports very frequent stools after constipation treatment despite normal tests; the other describes hypotonia, withholding, rashes and distress during repeated changes despite GI care. Both want experiences or help, and the latter also seeks commiseration and optimism.

- "We have not pushed potty training yet because the doctor wanted us to fully resolve the constipation issue and make sure she was comfortable first since potty training can sometimes cause constipation" [ar012-0141]
- "We won't ever get this time back with our sweet girl, and we're spending so much of it at home because being out is a nightmare so often." [ar009-0074]
- "Pooping = hurt = diaper changes = rash." [ar009-0078]
- "Oh and lastly, if anyone says "can grandma and grandpa come give you a break" - no, we live across the country and don't have a village here." [ar009-0081]

**But:** Frequent stool does not mean both children are visibly in pain. "She doesn't seem to be in any pain, it is honestly just a quality of life issue at this point" [ar012-0141]

Posts: 1v7kxly 1vv3a70

### They want reassurance about who can safely help their child with toileting.

1 of 12 posts.

A mother of a three-year-old worries about a male preschool teacher helping with accidents or bathroom visits. She connects her anxiety to her own childhood sexual abuse and describes discussing the duties with the director while already in trauma therapy. She wants grounding and other people's experiences, while her mother recommends moving daycares.

- "Due to my past history of childhood SA, I immediately felt anxious about this. I started imagining the worst." [ar051-0054]
- "The director said that they try to avoid that but that there may instances where he would." [ar051-0053]
- "My mom says I need to switch daycares, although, I know her reaction is based on her own trauma and mine." [ar051-0057]

**But:** She reports no existing complaints about the daycare. "I’ve had no complaints. A lot of the employees have their kids enrolled so it always felt like a safe environment." [ar051-0051]

Posts: 1v9i0xd

### They want dry naps and nights without making sleep harder.

1 of 12 posts.

A parent of a 27-month-old asks about overnight diapers after repeated soaked mornings. They have tried Millie Moon and Honest and are tired of washing sheets and the mattress cover. They had hoped for potty readiness by now but describe the opposite.

- "My 27 month old has been waking up soaked in pee several days a week" [ar061-0103]
- "I’m so tired of washing the sheets and mattress cover." [ar061-0103]
- "I’m using Millie moon overnight diapers. I’ve tried honest and those are even worse…." [ar061-0103]

**But:** nothing found against it.

Posts: 1w3ctev

### They worry their own anxiety, anger or delay is hurting potty learning.

1 of 12 posts.

A parent of a two-and-a-half-year-old describes being frightened to retry after a disastrous attempt just after age two. They blame a book for convincing them the child was ready and are now using another resource. They want advice, reassurance or solidarity and worry their own anxiety will affect the next attempt.

- "Let me make myself clear: the anxiety I’m experiencing over potty training is about ME, not my son." [ar003-0107]
- "I had read a book that had convinced me he was fully ready to train, when he actually was nowhere near ready" [ar003-0107]
- "My gut tells me my sweet boy is fully capable, but I am so worried/scared/anxious that I will fail him. 😓" [ar003-0108]

**But:** They now see readiness signs that were absent during the first attempt. "I am seeing signs of readiness in my son now at 2.5 that I did not see when we attempted training back in the spring." [ar003-0108]

