# Digest 13: pattern 11, Potty readiness and learning

Share: `baby-development/_work/2026-11/digests-age-reread/share-13.md`. Yes posts: 179. Near posts: 12.

## Pattern 11, share 13: yes posts

### They want a workable way to start potty training around the rest of family life.

15 of 179 posts.

Parents of two-year-olds and children approaching three ask where to begin, which rewards or equipment to use, and whether to train before or after a new baby. Pregnancy, travel, work schedules, another child's care and an antibiotic course complicate choosing a start date. Some have only introduced the potty; others are restarting after an earlier attempt or want a method that suits a sensitive child. A prospective relative foster carer also asks for basic toddler and potty guidance.

- "But then on the flip side if I wait until baby number 2 comes I might be too overwhelmed and it may be even harder to wait." [ar050-0001]
- "I bought the Hanes Boys' Potty Training Underwear, because we are planning on doing the cold turkey from diapers to underwear. But we don't have a full day or weekend to dedicate this to, based on our work and travel schedules." [ar044-0054]
- "I honestly don't even know where to start with potty training." [ar077-0001]
- "Starting potty training and would love some ideas for a potty treat basket that my 2.5 year old son would love (looking for more than just candy)!" [ar035-0001]
- "However this morning he was given antibiotics for a newly developed ear infection." [ar032-0044]

**But:** Starting from uncertainty does not always mean starting without any potty success. "M son has used the potty a few random times. He wakes with dry diapers and is showing signs of readiness." [ar081-0002]

Posts: 1s9spl9 1say8v8 1scm0u6 1ske27r 1smak6c 1so1es4 1t10th1 1t53qng 1tewyas 1tivvru 1uefjxp 1uismo4 1umnf9y 1v3s5ls 1wgjdid

### They ask whether to keep training or take a break when accidents and resistance overwhelm the attempt.

10 of 179 posts.

Parents of two-year-olds through newly three-year-olds describe first days or repeat attempts that bring crying, refusal, frequent accidents or no usable dry interval. They ask whether this is an expected learning period or a reason to stop and restart later. Some cannot sustain being confined at home, and one reports contradictory advice about constant urine dribbling.

- "Am I throwing in the towel too soon if I take a break? Should I keep going and push through the resistance? Or would it be better to stop for a few months and try again later?" [ar028-0134]
- "Now he’s even refusing to drink fluids" [ar037-0033]
- "I’m losing my patience and we have to go back to normal life soon—can’t just stay at home naked." [ar016-0036]
- "The problem is that she just keeps dribbling pee, one drop every half a minute or something." [ar059-0128]
- "I don't want to give up potty training but am not sure this is the method." [ar003-0116]

**But:** Some are seeing concrete progress alongside the distress. "But today shes communicated that she has had to go to the potty" [ar005-0024]

Posts: 1siv6hw 1slmzr6 1sw92tt 1txx113 1v12r6a 1v89elt 1vqfq35 1wmsuef 1wpx8je 1xa5qt2

### They see their child sit without releasing and then go elsewhere or wait for a diaper.

13 of 179 posts.

Parents of two- and three-year-olds describe children who can sit on a potty, know they need to go, or hold urine for hours, yet do not release there. Some wait for a nap or night diaper; others pee shortly after getting off, and one child continues holding even after diapers return. Parents try distraction, warmth, running water and rewards, while worrying about discomfort or harm.

- "After trying a new method that didn't work she now knows that she can hold her pee/poop from when she wakes up until she gets a nighttime diaper 12 hours later." [ar050-0007]
- "But once she sits, she just can't get herself to pee and poop. She will sit on it for ages, then get up and say she doesn't have to go anymore." [ar027-0088]
- "Now we have been back in diapers for 2 days and she is afraid to go pee or poop in her diaper." [ar029-0029]
- "right now he generally pees into a bottle and dumps it into the potty or sits on the potty in a diaper (put on moments before) for poop." [ar033-0139]
- "The problem I’m running into, is she will sit on the potty for 2-3 minutes, not go, and then pee during the 10 minute break." [ar066-0146]

**But:** One later update describes a successful restart after a break. "Dont lose hope for some reason the 3 month break just made something click in her brain!" [ar002-0096]

Posts: 1snhzhx 1sssq24 1sxbsys 1u1gh3b 1u58kpr 1ukr9fq 1vki85q 1w4s9mn 1w97aef 1wnrbum 1wye3l6 1x55vp8 1x9zszh

### They have tried several ways to teach the potty and still meet refusal or indifference.

14 of 179 posts.

Parents of children from about two and a half to nearly four list rewards, books, timers, underwear, naked time and repeated breaks without a lasting change. They describe diaper preference, fear of the toilet or dirty underwear, indifference to wetness, and resistance despite strong language or other skills. One considers training a younger sibling first; others compare the difficulty with an older child's easier training.

- "But it’s getting discouraging that it’s been so long and so many attempts with literally no progress at all!" [ar028-0157]
- "She will only sit on the potty if she has a diaper on and she won’t go anywhere near the training seat we got for the toilet." [ar075-0088]
- "I bought her an incentive basket with just a bunch of Target dollar tree items. She wants the items but won’t go potty." [ar079-0047]
- "Naked doesn’t work, peeing every 10 min doesn’t work, somehow he just saves liquid to go on the floor." [ar024-0086]
- "Every time I bring up that seat she says ‘she is going to fall down’" [ar069-0212]

