### They ask whether to keep training or take a break when accidents and resistance overwhelm the attempt.

10 of 179 posts.

Parents of two-year-olds through newly three-year-olds describe first days or repeat attempts that bring crying, refusal, frequent accidents or no usable dry interval. They ask whether this is an expected learning period or a reason to stop and restart later. Some cannot sustain being confined at home, and one reports contradictory advice about constant urine dribbling.

- "Am I throwing in the towel too soon if I take a break? Should I keep going and push through the resistance? Or would it be better to stop for a few months and try again later?" [ar028-0134]
- "Now he’s even refusing to drink fluids" [ar037-0033]
- "I’m losing my patience and we have to go back to normal life soon—can’t just stay at home naked." [ar016-0036]
- "The problem is that she just keeps dribbling pee, one drop every half a minute or something." [ar059-0128]
- "I don't want to give up potty training but am not sure this is the method." [ar003-0116]

**But:** Some are seeing concrete progress alongside the distress. "But today shes communicated that she has had to go to the potty" [ar005-0024]

