### They want dry naps and nights without making sleep harder.

7 of 179 posts.

Parents of children around two and a half through nearly four describe daytime toilet use with wet naps, soaked overnight pull-ups or renewed bedwetting. They ask whether dryness needs teaching or maturation and whether waking a poor sleeper is worth it. Preschool pressure to remove nap pull-ups appears in one account, while another describes abandoning an exhausting month of night training and seeking comfortable protection.

- "his preschool is close to kicking him out because they won’t allow pull-ups at naps and he is wetting himself basically everyday." [ar021-0135]
- "He is a terrible sleeper and needs help falling back asleep so I’m dreading waking him up to go, but know we need to start encouraging dry nights." [ar055-0062]
- "I’ve heard controlling it overnight is a developmental thing and then I’ve also heard people train overnight like it’s nothing." [ar041-0110]
- "It was hell for everyone, kid was miserable and exhausted and I was too." [ar037-0173]

**But:** One family reports a new reversal after months of dry nights, rather than never reaching dryness. "My now 3 year old, fully potty trained and has not wet the bed in over 5 months, has been bed wetting over the last 3 days." [ar028-0088]

Posts: 1sgqcz7 1toxpyo 1u3u12q 1uvf5wi 1vhkpkd 1vuzsp5 1wzom6o

### They want to know how much toileting their child can manage without their help.

4 of 179 posts.

Parents of three- and three-and-a-half-year-olds ask about independent wiping or getting to the bathroom alone at night. Wiping questions involve mess, dirty underwear from school and whether a parent is doing too much. Night questions concern bathroom access, waking parents and a safe setup outside the bedroom.

- "He wants to wipe his own bum. Which like…great. But it grosses me out and is so messy lol" [ar084-0022]
- "now it’s more relaxed and she’s pooping at school but coming home with very dirty underwear." [ar083-0056]
- "Our 3.5yo is new to waking at night to use the potty and I’m curious how long it took others to not wake parents every time, but rather do it on their own." [ar014-0063]
- "We’re contemplating taking the child lock off her door and letting her get up and go by herself if she needs to at night." [ar079-0154]

**But:** One parent says the child knows the wiping steps; the parent's concern is handling the soiled paper. "Yea I showed him how. He knows what to do. But gets a little wild with the toilet paper once it has poop lol" [ar084-0031]

