### They worry their own anxiety, anger or delay is hurting potty learning.

6 of 179 posts.

Parents of children aged two and a half through three, including one nearly four, focus on their own readiness, guilt or reactions. One autistic parent dreads bodily fluids and a meltdown; others feel they have procrastinated, failed a milestone or lost control while parenting under pressure. Two describe yelling or spanking in the wider parenting situation, with one explicitly asking how to repair trust after shaming a poop accident.

- "I'm autistic and the idea of bodily fluids on the floor or the furniture gives me a lot of irrational anxiety. I am afraid of melting down over accidents and messing up my son's progress." [ar061-0089]
- "And I think the failure is really on my part and not on hers." [ar041-0079]
- "I lost my cool and screamed at him that he was being a baby and that if he wants to poop in his underwear then it’s time to go back to diapers. I took his ruined underwear and threw in the trash in front of him." [ar007-0061]
- "I don’t want to resort to that and I truly want to do better for my children and I’ve been looking into some resources to help me be a better mom." [ar080-0024]
- "I don’t want to fail him." [ar052-0080]

**But:** One parent explicitly distinguishes spanking for other behaviour from potty accidents. "I do want to clarify that I don’t spank my child over potty accidents." [ar080-0024]

Posts: 1t7f1d2 1ti79s7 1tu9zdu 1vnb4tm 1x587h3 1x5groq

### They share progress after changing the approach to suit their child.

3 of 179 posts.

Parents of children around two and a half to three describe improvement after abandoning or modifying an approach that felt stressful. One prepared for a long time before low-pressure pantsless play; another switched from bottomless training to pull-ups and scheduled sits. A third used a poop-party story, treats and a short pause, and also describes apologising after an earlier angry response.

- "We just set him up and kind of let him potty train himself (with a little instruction and encouraging). And he figured it out!" [ar076-0100]
- "We then switched to wearing pull-ups and sitting on the potty every 30 mins this worked way better for all of us." [ar057-0104]
- "By himself, he took himself and pooped ON THE POTTY." [ar055-0114]
- "After he woke up, I apologized and told him I was wrong." [ar055-0116]

**But:** The longer low-pressure account still has a separate poop hurdle. "I will say we do still need to figure out pooping on the potty. He's pretty freaked out about that." [ar076-0101]

Posts: 1tzhur4 1wks1w5 1wnowa5

### They want potty teaching that works with their child's current communication.

4 of 179 posts.

Parents of two-year-olds, including children aged 29 and 34 months, ask whether limited speech or autism changes how to teach toileting. They describe understanding, words or diaper awareness alongside difficulty announcing a need before going. Two identify their child as autistic, one non-speaking; stripping and removing a wet diaper are treated by the parent as possible signs, not proof of readiness.

- "any advice you can give me to help me teach my son to learn potty training while not completely verbal just yet and while I’m also taking care of a 5 week old newborn." [ar040-0023]
- "I’m worried about how not communicating in full sentences yet would affect potty training?" [ar066-0092]
- "He has autism, and doesn’t speak." [ar028-0152]
- "she can’t tell the difference between poop and gas, and can’t/wont tell us before she needs to go, only after she’s done." [ar080-0098]

**But:** Limited sentences do not mean an absence of words or understanding in every account. "he has an extensive vocabulary and is now starting to put two words together, he has knowledge and understanding." [ar066-0092]

